  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 244 PM MST WED NOV 25 2026\nOn the heels of the shortwave that moved through, upper-level \nridging will bring high pressure Saturday and Sunday. Some \nlingering showers across SW Idaho will be ongoing to start \nSaturday, but will quickly taper off as dry conditions dominate \nboth days. This dry period will be short-lived though, as \nanother upper-level shortwave trough is forecast to move across \nthe Pacific Northwest to start next week. Precipitation chances \nwill begin to rise Sunday night with another push of valley \nrain and mountain snow. This system will bring slightly cooler \ntemperatures and lower snow levels back down to 5000-6000 ft \nMSL. Ridging will attempt to build back in early Wednesday, but \na closed low just off the California coast will move through the\nregion, splitting the upper-level flow and bringing cooler air \nand precipitation back to the southern part of the area. Model \nuncertainty remains on the timing and positioning of the low, \nbut most solutions and ensemble members agree that the bulk of \nthe precipitation will be to our south over the Desert Southwest.\nCurrent forecast keeps a 20-40% chance of precipitation over \nthe region through Wednesday, with temperatures lingering around\n5-10 degrees above normal. \n\nThe potential for a hard freeze in the Boise metro area appears to \nstill not occur by early December. This would be the latest freeze \non record, with the previous latest freeze occurring November 16th,\n2016.",
